# FlyRank Capstone — Development Rules

## Tech Stack
- Frontend: React / Next.js
- Styling: Tailwind CSS
- Language: TypeScript

## Code Style & Conventions
- Use clean, modular, functional components — no class components.
- Prioritize semantic HTML and accessible UI design.
- Keep components small and single-purpose.
- Follow Conventional Commits for all repository updates.
- Component files use PascalCase (e.g., `UserCard.tsx`).
- Use hooks for state management.

## Folder Structure
- `app/` — routes, layouts, and route-local components (Next.js App Router)
- `components/` — shared, reusable UI components
- `lib/` — shared types, utilities, and hooks

## Notes for AI Assistant
- This project is early-stage — ask clarifying questions before assuming architecture or features.
- Explain reasoning before making changes.
- Prioritize maintainability over cleverness.

## Lessons Learned from AI-assisted workflow drill 

- Always verify export/import style matches when a component is generated or 
  edited by AI — check whether the file uses `export default` or a named 
  `export`, and confirm the importing file matches. A mismatch causes a runtime 
  crash that only appears when the component is 
  actually rendered.

- When AI generates a test file, verify its relative import paths against the 
  actual project structure before running it — AI often assumes a `__tests__/` 
  or nested folder convention (e.g., `../Component`) even when the test file is 
  placed directly alongside the component (`./Component`).

- A prompt that names a specific requirement (e.g., "with validation") is not 
  automatically vague, even if it's short — the AI will reach for whatever 
  conventions already exist in `.cursor/rules/`. Precise prompts should instead 
  specify exact scope (fields, behavior) and an explicit verification step 
  ("write it, then write tests and run them") to control what gets built and 
  confirm it actually works.